Clamp pointer motion only by a rounding epsilon, not to the window edge.

pointer_handle_motion_internal() clamped pointer coordinates to the focused
window's rect. The upstream comment says this is for coordinates "slightly
outside ... due to rounding", but the clamp was UNCONDITIONAL: motion any
distance outside the window was pinned to that window's border.

Wayland has an implicit pointer grab -- while a button is held, pointer focus
stays on the surface that received the press and the compositor keeps reporting
motion far outside it. Pinning those coordinates makes Wine believe the cursor
never left the source window, so every drag that STARTS in a popup or secondary
surface dies: the drop target never sees the pointer.

Measured symptom: Premiere's Program Monitor "Button Editor" highlights entries
on hover (in-bounds motion, never clamped) but a button cannot be dragged onto
the button bar. Drags entirely within one toplevel (project bin -> timeline)
share a surface, never leave window_rect, and were never affected -- which is
why this survived so long.

Clamp only an overshoot small enough to be a rounding artifact. The epsilon must
cover fractional-scale rounding: coords_to_window() multiplies by the surface
scale, so a 0.5 surface-px error is up to scale/2 window px (~1.13 at 2.25).
2 px covers it; anything beyond is a real position and must be passed through so
win32u can route it to the capture window.

NOTE: dlls/winewayland.drv/wintab.c carries an identical copy of this clamp for
the injected tablet cursor (from neutron-wintab.mypatch) and is predicted to
break pen drags out of a popup the same way. Deliberately NOT changed here so
this fix can be tested without perturbing the tablet path.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c	2026-08-12 17:30:13.293733885 -0400
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c	2026-08-12 17:30:13.272578638 -0400
@@ -128,6 +128,10 @@
 static BOOL wayland_pointer_show_shape(enum wp_cursor_shape_device_v1_shape shape);
 static volatile LONG *neutron_cursor_shm(void);
 
+/* NEUTRON: max overshoot (in window px) still treated as a rounding artifact by
+ * pointer_handle_motion_internal(). See the comment at the clamp below. */
+#define NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS 2
+
 static void pointer_handle_motion_internal(wl_fixed_t sx, wl_fixed_t sy)
 {
     INPUT input = {0};
@@ -154,11 +158,49 @@
     screen.x += window_rect->left;
     screen.y += window_rect->top;
     /* Sometimes, due to rounding, we may end up with pointer coordinates
-     * slightly outside the target window, so bring them within bounds. */
-    if (screen.x >= window_rect->right) screen.x = window_rect->right - 1;
-    else if (screen.x < window_rect->left) screen.x = window_rect->left;
-    if (screen.y >= window_rect->bottom) screen.y = window_rect->bottom - 1;
-    else if (screen.y < window_rect->top) screen.y = window_rect->top;
+     * slightly outside the target window, so bring them within bounds.
+     *
+     * 🚨 NEUTRON: clamp ONLY a ROUNDING-SIZED overshoot, never an arbitrary one.
+     * The clamp is upstream and its comment says "slightly outside", but it was
+     * unconditional: motion ANY distance outside the focused window was pinned
+     * to that window's edge. Wayland has an IMPLICIT POINTER GRAB -- while a
+     * button is held, pointer focus stays on the surface that received the
+     * press and the compositor keeps reporting motion far outside it. Pinning
+     * those coordinates makes Wine believe the cursor never left the source
+     * window, so EVERY drag that starts in a popup or secondary surface dies:
+     * the drop target never sees the pointer. Measured symptom (2026-08-12):
+     * Premiere's Program Monitor "Button Editor" highlights on hover but a
+     * button cannot be dragged onto the button bar.
+     *
+     * Drags entirely WITHIN one toplevel (project bin -> timeline) share a
+     * surface, never leave window_rect, and were never affected -- which is why
+     * this survived so long.
+     *
+     * The epsilon must cover fractional-scale rounding: coords_to_window()
+     * multiplies by surface->window.scale, so a 0.5 surface-px rounding error
+     * is up to scale/2 window px (~1.13 at the 2.25 scale a build tester runs).
+     * 2 px covers it with margin; anything beyond that is a real position and
+     * MUST be passed through so win32u can route it to the capture window. */
+    if (screen.x >= window_rect->right)
+    {
+        if (screen.x - window_rect->right < NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen.x = window_rect->right - 1;
+    }
+    else if (screen.x < window_rect->left)
+    {
+        if (window_rect->left - screen.x <= NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen.x = window_rect->left;
+    }
+    if (screen.y >= window_rect->bottom)
+    {
+        if (screen.y - window_rect->bottom < NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen.y = window_rect->bottom - 1;
+    }
+    else if (screen.y < window_rect->top)
+    {
+        if (window_rect->top - screen.y <= NEUTRON_PTR_EDGE_EPS)
+            screen.y = window_rect->top;
+    }
 
     wayland_win_data_release(data);
 
